text:
rachel: Quick RFC follow-up: prototype for an adaptive 'lease-rebalance' controller that coordinates reuse reservoirs, preserve windows, and the evictor. Goal: keep hot spans warm while bounding fragmentation for multi-tenant bursts. Short thread for tradeoffs + a minimal API.

omid: Can you describe the inputs? Which signals drive decisions?

rachel: Controller samples per-pool signals: alloc.fail_rate, alloc.large_failures, tail_p95/99, queue_depth, reuse.hit_rate, preserved.occupancy, and tenant_scoped pressure. It outputs adjustments to reuse_window, preserve_ms, and a 'reclaim_pressure' token that signals evictor to be aggressive.

tara: How does it avoid thrashing (oscillation) across many pools?

rachel: Simple rules + hysteresis. We only change settings if delta exceeds thresholds for N ticks (default N=3). Also clamp change rate and prefer conservative steps. Controller exposes a versioned decision stamp so operators can trace changes.

jules: Interaction with evictor: what does reclaim_pressure do?

rachel: reclaim_pressure is a 0..1 float. Evictor uses it to boost eviction priority for preserved/reuse-reservoir entries when reclaim_pressure > 0.5. This lets large allocation requests force reclamation of preserved holes. Evictor still respects tenant quotas.

omid: So large allocs can preempt reuse, good. Implementation detail: how do we atomically transfer a span from reuse reservoir back to free list without races?

rachel: Use atomic CAS on span header with a small state machine: FREE -> REUSE_RESERVED -> ALLOC. reclaim path takes REUSE_RESERVED via CAS; if CAS fails, fallback to normal alloc path. Reservoir metadata is advisory.

sre: Operational guardrails? Dedicated/private differences?

rachel: Defaults: Hosted: controller enabled, moderate aggressiveness. Dedicated: controller disabled by default (opt-in); Private: opt-in. Add a global kill-switch runtime.runtime.rebalance.enabled. Also add per-pool override.

jules: Telemetry list pls.

rachel: Will emit: rebalance.decisions (event), rebalance.reuse_window, rebalance.preserve_ms, rebalance.reclaim_pressure, reuse.hits/misses, preserved.hits/evicted, alloc.fail_rate, alloc.large_failures, evict.reclaimed_bytes_by_reclaim_pressure. Also expose /debug/pools/<id>/rebalance to show last 10 decisions.

omid: API sketch?

rachel: Pseudocode: 

/* controller tick */
void rebalance_tick(pool) {
  m = sample_metrics(pool);
  if (m.alloc_large_failures > pool.cfg.fail_thresh) {
    pool.cfg.reuse_window *= 0.7; pool.cfg.preserve_ms = pool.cfg.min_preserve;
    pool.reclaim_pressure = min(1.0, pool.reclaim_pressure + 0.25);
  } else if (m.tail_p99 < pool.cfg.lat_target && m.reuse_hit_rate < pool.cfg.hit_target) {
    pool.cfg.reuse_window = min(pool.cfg.max_reuse, pool.cfg.reuse_window * 1.05);
    pool.reclaim_pressure = max(0.0, pool.reclaim_pressure - 0.05);
  }
  emit_decision_event(pool);
}

/* evictor uses reclaim_pressure */
span_id evictor_select(pool, size) {
  candidates = gather_candidates(pool);
  score = base_score(candidate);
  score += candidate.in_reservoir ? pool.reclaim_pressure * RESERVOIR_PENALTY : 0;
  return top_score(candidate);
}

sre: We should add alerts when controller flips frequently or makes large adjustments. Also record rolling window to debug incidents.

jules: What about tenant fairness? If one tenant is noisy, controller could shrink reuse globally and hurt other tenants. Better to compute tenant-scoped decisions when tenant id is present.

rachel: Agreed. Controller runs per-pool but maintains tenant-scoped sub-controllers if tenant labels exist. Top-level falls back to pool-level aggregates. We also enforce per-tenant reservoir caps so one tenant can't monopolize preserved spans.

omid: Testing plan?

rachel: Three stages: microbench with synthetic bursts per-tenant (validate tail latency and host-alloc reduction), stress with many tenants churn + forced large allocs (verify no retention of huge preserved bytes), and canary rollout with telemetry thresholds. Add fuzz tests for CAS races and a unit test for the evictor scoring change.

sre: Also a controlled fault injection: make controller flip every tick and verify kill-switch respected.

jules: One nit: keep controller tick >= 200ms to avoid too-frequent oscillation and reduce load.

rachel: Will default to 250ms. Any objections to the overall approach?

omid: :thumbsup: sounds reasonable. Please include knobs and a migration guide in PR.

jules: +1. Add a short explainer doc for SRE on how to reason about reclaim_pressure.

sre: And include runbook steps: how to set runtime.runtime.rebalance.enabled=false and revert.

evict-bot: rebalance.tick scheduled for pool gpu-7 every 250ms :gear: 

rachel: I'll open RFC-followup PR with controller code, decision events, telemetry, tests, and a canary plan. Will ping for reviews. Thanks all.
